      id: 'l8', key: 'aa-az-8', title: 'Cost, throughput, and monitoring for document pipelines', concept: 'aa-az-doc-ops',
      story: "Document pipelines have spiky, predictable loads: month-end invoice runs, claim surges after storms, onboarding waves after a campaign. Teams that sized their pipelines on daily averages watched queues grow for days at month end. Planning for pages per hour, service rate limits, and per-page prices, and monitoring the whole flow end to end, turned document processing from a recurring firefight into a routine.",
      analogy: "A bakery planning for the Saturday morning rush, not the average Tuesday afternoon.",
      timeline: [['2019', 'Per-page pricing', 'Cloud document services charge per page analysed.'], ['2023', 'Add-on pricing', 'Premium features such as high-resolution OCR priced separately.'], ['2024', 'Batch analysis', 'APIs for analysing many documents in one operation.'], ['2025–2026', 'End-to-end observability', 'Pipelines monitored from upload to export.']],
      explain: "**Cost** is mostly pages × price per 1,000 pages for the chosen model (read is far cheaper than prebuilt or custom extraction), plus add-ons (high resolution, formulas, query fields), custom model training, storage, queues, compute, and any LLM calls in post-processing. **Throughput** depends on concurrency, document size, service-side processing time, and your resource's **transactions-per-second limits**, which you can raise through support requests. Estimate pages per hour = concurrent workers × (3,600 ÷ seconds per document) × pages per document, and compare with peak arrivals. **Monitoring** covers the flow: queue depth and age at each stage, analyze latency and errors (including 429s), straight-through rate, review backlog, and cost per document. Reduce cost by using read or layout where they suffice, classifying first so expensive models run only on the right pages, skipping blank or duplicate pages, and caching results by document hash.",
      diagram: "pages/hour = workers × (3600 ÷ seconds per document) × pages per document\n10 × (3600 ÷ 12) × 4 = 12,000 pages/hour\ncost/hour  = pages/hour ÷ 1000 × price per 1,000 pages\n12,000 ÷ 1000 × $10 = $120/hour   (illustrative price; check the pricing page)",
