      deepDive: {
        explain: "Measure before scaling: run a load test with representative documents to get seconds per document and error rates at increasing concurrency; past some point you will hit 429s from the service's rate limits, and retries with backoff will flatten throughput. Plan downstream too: human review capacity, ERP import limits, and LLM quota for post-processing often bottleneck before OCR does. Use **autoscaling workers** driven by queue length (for example KEDA on Container Apps or AKS), with a maximum concurrency below the service limit. Track **cost per document** and **cost per straight-through document** by type; a model that is cheaper per page but sends more documents to review may cost more overall once reviewer time is included. Keep dashboards that join pipeline metrics with business outcomes: invoices paid on time, claims settled, backlog age.",
        example: ['js', 'pages per hour and cost per hour', "function pagesPerHour(c) {\n  const pages = c.concurrency * 3600 / c.secondsPerDoc * c.pagesPerDoc;\n  return { pagesPerHour: Math.round(pages), costPerHour: Math.round(pages / 1000 * c.pricePer1000Pages * 100) / 100 };\n}"],
        tradeoffs: [['Lever', 'Saves', 'Risk'], ['Cheaper model where sufficient', 'Per-page cost', 'Missing structure or fields'], ['Classify first', 'Avoids expensive models on wrong pages', 'Classifier errors'], ['Higher concurrency', 'Throughput', '429s, downstream overload'], ['Queue-based autoscaling', 'Matches load', 'Cold starts; tuning']],
        failures: [['Month-end backlog lasts days', 'Capacity planned on averages', 'Plan for peak; autoscale on queue length'], ['Throughput flat despite more workers', 'Service rate limits (429s)', 'Respect limits; request increases; backoff'], ['Bill spikes with no volume change', 'High-resolution add-on enabled for all', 'Enable add-ons only where needed'], ['OCR fast but invoices still late', 'Review team is the bottleneck', 'Monitor review backlog; raise straight-through rate']],
      },
      warn: "Scaling workers past the service's rate limits does not increase throughput; it multiplies 429s and retries. Cap concurrency and request higher limits instead.",
